 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are delivered over self-hosted WebSockets: the server holds a connection per browser and pushes each committed event revision as it lands, with no third-party realtime service in the path. Subsecond updates would be useful but are not required; the socket path meets the freshness target with headroom.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around a pushed, replayable revision stream. The change store keeps event revisions as a replayable sequence and publishes committed revisions reliably; delivery owns socket session management and publication to connected browsers; browser sync owns subscription, reconnect, and catch-up after a gap. The board owns its connection capacity and reconnect handling.
 
